A domain for a ramp that runs out from a neutral middle rather than up from nothing.

A diverging scale's middle stop means "neither", and it only says that where the values
change sign. The map's own `valueDomain` runs from zero to the largest value, which would
put that neutral at half the maximum and paint the smallest area in the far negative
colour - a map of quarters between 15% and 40% would read as though half the city were in
deficit. So a diverging palette works out a domain of its own.

`valueDomain` is left as it is rather than widened, because the bubbles read it too and a
radius scale handed a negative domain draws circles with negative radii.

// #hidden

// #region stateTypes
colorDomain: [number, number];
// #endregion

// #region init
// NOTE: The larger of the two extremes, mirrored, so that a value and its negative sit the
// same distance from the neutral middle - which is what makes the two sides comparable.
// An area with no number is skipped here as it is skipped on the map.
const amplitude = d3.max(data, (d) => Math.abs(d.value)) ?? 0;
state.colorDomain = [-amplitude, amplitude];
// #endregion
